    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Sore throat, runny nose and cough for three days during chemotherapy. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Sun called the clinic and was seen the same day. Three days ago he developed a scratchy throat and a runny nose after a week of meetings, and since yesterday a dry cough. He checked his temperature twice a day as taught and the highest was 37.5 °C; he has had no shivering. He is on day 10 of cycle 5 of gemcitabine, cisplatin and durvalumab and had his day-8 infusion two days ago. He is not short of breath, even on the stairs, and has no chest pain, diarrhea, rash, or new abdominal pain. His appetite is a little lower but he is drinking well. Two colleagues at work have colds. He asked whether this could be pneumonitis from durvalumab and whether he needs antibiotics. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago, after colleagues at work had colds</td></tr><tr><td>Symptoms</td><td>Sore throat, runny nose, dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Highest 37.5 °C at home; no shivering</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment context</td><td>Day 10 of cycle 5 of gemcitabine, cisplatin and durvalumab; day-8 dose two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No breathlessness, chest pain, diarrhea, rash, or new abdominal pain</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Warm drinks and honey help the throat</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

