<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.5" extension="2015-08-01"/><templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.5.1" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Right upper quadrant abdominal mass with weight loss: a dull ache under the right ribs and early fullness for about three months, 4 kg weight loss, and a firm, non-tender mass felt 4 cm below the right costal margin; no jaundice, fever, or itching. Ultrasound today: a 6.8 cm solid, heterogeneous mass in liver segments IV and V that cannot be separated from the gallbladder, which contains stones; the rest of the liver is smooth, with no ascites. Multiphase CT, tumor markers, hepatitis serology and medical oncology consultation arranged</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R19.01</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to the documented right upper abdominal mass; superseded by the biopsy diagnosis.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Adenocarcinoma of the gallbladder invading the liver: core biopsy of the liver mass shows moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma (CK7 positive, CK20 and CDX2 negative), and multiphase CT and MRI show a 7.1 cm mass arising from the gallbladder fundus and invading liver segments IVb and V, with gallstones and two enlarged nodes in the hepatoduodenal ligament; no cirrhosis; alpha-fetoprotein normal, CA 19-9 raised. Clinical T3 N1 before staging laparoscopy (AJCC 8th edition). Synthetic modern episode linked to his documented final illness, described in life as liver cancer and at autopsy as gallbladder adenocarcinoma</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C23</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis chosen to fit the documented findings: a mass involving the liver at operation and gallbladder adenocarcinoma at autopsy.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Resolved hepatitis B infection: hepatitis B surface antigen negative, total core antibody positive, surface antibody positive, HBV DNA undetectable; hepatitis C antibody negative. At risk of reactivation during chemotherapy and immunotherapy; monitored</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Z86.19</td><td>S — Synthetic serology; not a claim that he had hepatitis B in his lifetime. No fetched source mentions hepatitis.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Peritoneal metastases from gallbladder adenocarcinoma: several 3 to 6 mm nodules on the parietal peritoneum and omentum at staging laparoscopy, biopsy positive for adenocarcinoma of the same type; stage IVB (M1), not resectable</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C78.6</td><td>S — Synthetic finding anchored to the peritoneal metastases reported at autopsy.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Acute viral upper respiratory infection on day 10 of cycle 5 of chemoimmunotherapy: sore throat, runny nose and dry cough for three days, afebrile, with a neutrophil count of 1.6 x10^3/uL; rapid tests for SARS-CoV-2 and influenza negative; no sign of pneumonia or pneumonitis; resolved</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J06.9</td><td>S — Synthetic acute illness; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

