    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection (J06.9)</content>. A cold caught from colleagues, with mild neutropenia (1.6 x10^3/uL) expected two days after a day-8 dose and no fever. Rapid SARS-CoV-2 and influenza tests are negative. Pneumonitis from durvalumab is unlikely: the cough is dry and new with cold symptoms, he is not breathless, the lungs are clear, and his oxygen saturation is normal and holds on walking. He has no fever, so this is not febrile neutropenia, and antibiotics would not help. [S]<list><item>Fluids, rest, warm drinks with honey, and saline nasal rinses.</item><item>Acetaminophen as already prescribed; it may mask a fever, so check the temperature before each dose.</item><item>Call the oncology line at once and go to the emergency department for a temperature of 38.0 °C or higher, shivering, breathlessness, chest pain, or oxygen saturation below 94% on his home meter.</item><item>Phone check in two days; blood count before cycle 6 as booked.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gallbladder adenocarcinoma, stage IVB, on chemoimmunotherapy (C23)</content>. Cycle 6 can go ahead on schedule if the cold has settled and the neutrophil count has recovered, as is usual by day 21. [S]<list><item>Dr. Choi&apos;s team informed.</item><item>Cycle 6 day 1 as booked in twelve days.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Biliary Tract Cancers (2026); Management of Immune-Related Adverse Events in Patients Treated With Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy: ASCO Guideline Update (2021); Outpatient Management of Fever and Neutropenia in Adults Treated for Malignancy: American Society of Clinical Oncology and Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guideline Update (2018)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Phone check in two days. Cycle 6 day 1 as booked, with the oncology team; preventive visit in about three weeks. Next scheduled visit 1924-09-21: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
